  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem</p><h2>A score is a moment.<br />Progress needs context.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>As a competitive skeet shooter, I wanted to see more than individual results. I wanted to understand performance across gauges, the events contributing to my rolling average, and the history behind a classification.</p><p>Skeet Tracker brings those questions into a phone-friendly application for National Skeet Shooting Association (NSSA) registered scores. It covers 12, 20, and 28 gauge, .410 bore, and optional doubles.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / My approach</p><h2>Model the sport.<br />Then simplify the view.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>I shaped the product around the way a tournament actually unfolds: several events, sometimes spread over multiple days, with results arriving at different times. The application’s job is to preserve those details while making the next useful piece of information easy to find.</p><p>My role spans the requirements, user experience, data model, implementation, and iteration. The resulting product connects a personal need to hands-on engineering work.</p></div></section>
  <div class="insight-grid"><article><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Events stay distinct</h3><p>Preliminary and main scores remain separate registered events. History can combine their totals without losing the sequence needed for rolling classifications.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Incomplete isn’t zero</h3><p>A shoot can remain in progress. Completed scores update the relevant averages; blank or pending events stay out of the calculation.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Show the evidence</h3><p>The interface shows the five recent events behind each average, alongside the class and tournament history, so the summary has context.</p></article></div>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Architecture</p><h2>A focused application<br />with durable storage.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The public repository uses React with Next.js-compatible routing through Vinext, Cloudflare Worker API routes, and Cloudflare D1 / SQLite persistence. Drizzle manages the data model and versioned migrations.</p><p>Each deployment has an independent database. The repository contains schema and migrations rather than personal score history.</p></div></section>
  <div class="architecture-flow" role="img" aria-label="Browser interface built with React and Vinext calls Cloudflare Worker API routes, which persist scores through Drizzle into Cloudflare D1 and SQLite"><div><span>Experience</span><strong>React / Vinext</strong><p>Scores, averages & history</p></div><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><div><span>Application</span><strong>Cloudflare Worker</strong><p>API routes</p></div><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><div><span>Persistence</span><strong>D1 / SQLite</strong><p>Drizzle & migrations</p></div></div>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Decisions & tradeoffs</p><h2>Keep the detail that<br />changes the answer.</h2></div><div class="prose"><h3>One shoot, multiple views</h3><p>Tournament history needs aggregate totals. Classification needs individual events in order. Modeling those separately supports both views without treating them as the same calculation.</p><h3>Useful before a tournament is finished</h3><p>Completed events can update displayed averages during a shoot. The repository separately handles when preliminary results affect official classification and rank notifications.</p><h3>A small deployment footprint</h3><p>Worker routes and D1 keep the application and persistence within Cloudflare. That is a concrete hosting dependency: another provider would need a compatible persistence adapter.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-callout"><p class="eyebrow">What this demonstrates</p><h2>Product judgment grounded<br />in the domain.</h2><p>Taking a real user problem through requirements, data modeling, interface design, and working software—and preserving the edge cases that make the result useful.</p></section>
